1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Sámi saw — a hunt that
isn’t over yet.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The Sámi live in the far north of
Norway, Sweden and Finland, and on
Russia’s Kola Peninsula.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Few Sámi constellations survive, but
they all tell one story.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
A giant bull reindeer (some say a moose)
from Cassiopeia’s W through Perseus to
Capella.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
In late 2025, the IAU named two Perseus
stars: Sarvvis and Áldu — bull and cow
reindeer.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Across the sky is Arcturus. His bow is
the Big Dipper, aimed north at the
reindeer.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It sits right between them: Boahjenásti,
the star that holds up the sky.

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Legend says that if Fávdna’s arrow hits
it, the sky falls and the world ends.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
So every night the bow is drawn, but the
arrow waits. The hunt is not over yet.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Siberian hunters see a moose; the Inuit
see a caribou. For the Sámi, it’s a
hunter’s bow.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Northern skies: Arcturus in the east,
the bow northeast, the reindeer
northwest — the Pole Star in between.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Belarus: a farmer’s sky and Elijah’s
chariot.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

